#SRECaseStudy - Stuck Jenkins Builds Due to Disk Pressure

🛠️ Incident
Developers reported that Jenkins builds were stuck in the “Pending” state for over 30 minutes during a production deployment window. Build agents showed “Offline” and new jobs couldn’t start.

🔍 Investigation
- kubectl get nodes revealed one worker node in NotReady state with a DiskPressure taint.
- kubectl describe node <node> confirmed that /var/lib/kubelet was at 95% disk usage.
- Jenkins pod logs showed repeated No space left on device errors.

⚙️ Root Cause
- The node hosting Jenkins agents ran out of disk space because old Docker images and logs accumulated over time.
- Kubernetes tainted the node with DiskPressure, preventing new pods from scheduling.

🛠️ Resolution
1️⃣ Cleaned unused Docker images & containers:

docker system prune -af

2️⃣ Increased node disk size in the cloud provider (AWS EBS volume).
3️⃣ Implemented a log rotation policy and set up a cron job to run docker system prune weekly.
4️⃣ Added node disk usage alerts in Prometheus/Grafana.

📚 Lessons Learned
✅ Regularly monitor node disk usage with alerts.
✅ Implement automated cleanup for Docker artifacts.
✅ Ensure critical services like Jenkins have persistent storage separate from system disks.
